+- Local branches and worktrees whose work has landed (see below).
+#### Pruning finished branches and worktrees
+
+Finished branches pile up after every merged pull request. The `branches` group removes them for you, using the pull request state from your VCS as the ground truth (not `git branch -d` or `git cherry`, which cannot see squash merges).
+
+```bash
+ta cleanup --only branches --dry-run   # List each branch, the verdict and the reason. Changes nothing.
+ta cleanup --only branches             # Delete the safe ones, ask about the rest
+```
+
+A local branch is deleted without asking only when its pull request is merged and the branch has no commits beyond the PR head, or when its tip is already contained in the default branch. Everything else becomes a question and is kept until you answer:
+
+- the PR is still open, was closed without merging, or no PR exists
+- the branch has commits after the PR head
+- the PR state could not be read
+- the branch is checked out in a worktree with uncommitted or untracked files (the question lists the files, and the only offered answer is to keep it)
+
+The current branch and the default branch are never touched. A finished branch that is checked out in a clean worktree is removed together with that worktree. Remote branches are never deleted. Remote branches whose PR was closed unmerged are listed as notes so you can remove them on the host.
+
+Every deletion is logged in `.ta/audit.jsonl` with the branch name and its full tip SHA. To bring a pruned branch back: `git branch <name> <tip-sha>`.
+
+This runs on its own: right after `ta draft watch` sees a draft's pull request merge, and in the daemon's periodic and startup cleanup (even when `auto = "off"`). Turn it off with `prune_branches = false`.
+
+prune_branches = true # Prune merged branches and worktrees after a merge and in scheduled cleanup (default on)
